  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Testing GLB Creation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on testing the output of `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` for `concrete-fence-1a.glb`. I plan to write a small Node.js script to examine the results of this process. My immediate goal is to understand what transformations or assets this function generates. The focus is on verifying and understanding the specifics of the GLB asset.\n\n\n**Analyzing GLB Structure**\n\nI\'ve discovered an interesting discrepancy in the GLB structure. I found that while `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` exists and includes `Plane.20009` and `cuboid_fixed.009` as children, nodes like `ConcreteFence_1A.001` through `ConcreteFence_1A.005` are root nodes, not children of the REF node. This behavior isn\'t what I had anticipated. I\'m reviewing the node traversal logic, specifically lines 2139-2155, to understand how `refNodes` are identified and selected, in the GLB file.\n\n\n**Considering Fence Placement**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to implement the user\'s request to replace barricade walls with the `concrete-fence-1a.glb` model around the \'Blue Base\' and \'Red Base\'. I need to ensure edge-to-edge placement while maintaining the original sub-mesh structure, which could mean handling multiple instances of the GLB. The key consideration is the placement of this model as a replacement for existing walls.\n\n\n**Evaluating Wall Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the existing perimeter wall\'s placement code. I\'ve noted the existing `place` calls using \'concrete\\_fence\' and I am examining the spacing used in their placement. I need to understand the pattern to correctly apply the fence to the new base perimeter. It looks like the spacing is -6 units each. I\'m reviewing the existing base coordinates and wall lengths.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
