    thinking: "**Analyzing Base Coordinates**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the exact HQ coordinates: Blue\'s is at (-46.5, 39.0), Red\'s at (46.5, -39.0). I\'m currently cross-referencing these with the bastion placements in the `buildMilitaryBaseMasterplan`. The bastions are at [-10.0, -16.5], [19.0, -16.5], [-10.0, 16.5], [19.0, 16.5].\n\n\n**Calculating Base Perimeter**\n\nI\'ve determined the exact dimensions of the military base perimeter.  The X-coordinates range from -56.5 to -27.5 (width of 29.0), while the Z-coordinates span from 22.5 to 55.5 (depth of 33.0). The bastions are confirmed to be at the four corners. I\'m now cross-referencing this information with the perimeter wall specifications in `buildTeamBaseQuadrant`.\n\n\n**Defining Perimeter Walls**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the perimeter wall placements, specifically the concrete fence segments. I\'m verifying the placement coordinates and orientations, including the north, south, west, and east walls. Currently, I\'m examining how each wall aligns within the established base dimensions and verifying fence segment lengths.\n\n\n**Verifying Wall Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the northern perimeter wall, calculating fence segment placements. I\'m verifying the alignment of each concrete fence segment and cross-referencing this against the bastion radii. It looks like the fence and bastion radii do not match! The fences are under-specified to meet the required distance. I will need to refine these parameters to match.\n\n\n**Refining Fence Placement**\n\nI\'ve got a clearer picture of the wall placement problems now. The North and South walls are dramatically underspecified: the available fence segments only cover about 10 meters of required perimeter. The West wall has a 29.4-meter run, and only 10 meters of fence. For the East wall, the gate adds complexity. I\'m taking a hard look at the \"sub-mesh\" structure, cross-referencing this against the model `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, to ensure complete perimeter coverage. I am now considering how to account for this gate entrance using fence segments. The barricade props present a new element to integrate.\n\n\n**Investigating Fence Meshes**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the individual meshes within `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, trying to understand their function. I noticed multiple fence segments, and I\'m cross-referencing their translations to see how they fit together. I\'ve pinpointed their X-axis offsets, calculating the distances between them and noticing that the distances are not consistent, which is unexpected. I\'m now verifying the exact mesh structure to clarify why there are multiple meshes in the first place, and where this might impact the perimeter coverage problem I found earlier.\n\n\n**Examining Mesh Instantiation**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the game engine spawns props, particularly focusing on the `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` function. I\'m cross-referencing this against the mesh structure in `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, especially the `ref` nodes. It seems that the engine might be selecting a base reference node and any siblings with the same footprint. I need to understand how the engine interprets the `translation`, `scale`, and `mesh` data for each segment and how this ties into the perimeter coverage.\n\n\n**Interpreting Mesh Instantiation**\n\nI\'m digging deeper into how meshes are spawned, particularly with `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb`. I see the code clones meshes and calculates relative positions based on the source\'s matrix. It also applies material upgrades and stroke meshes. This process is crucial because it suggests the game engine handles each fence segment as an individual mesh, with relative transformations. I now understand that this confirms my prior findings about the fence segment problems being caused by individual mesh instantiation, not overall dimensions.\n\n\n**Analyzing Fence Models**\n\nI\'m now investigating the differences between `concrete-fence-1a.glb` and `concrete-fence-1b.glb` to understand which fence segment the engine uses. I\'ve confirmed that the game engine treats each fence segment as a separate mesh. I\'m focusing on their internal structure to understand how they contribute to the perimeter coverage and to determine if there are any significant differences in their geometries. I\'m now comparing the mesh data of these models.\n\n\n"
