    thinking: "**Analyzing Mesh Cloning**\n\nI\'ve been examining `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` and noticed its strategy for preserving sub-mesh structure. Essentially, for each child mesh, the function clones individual `THREE.Mesh` objects using `new THREE.Mesh(cleanGeo, srcMesh.material)`. It also copies the name property. This targeted cloning approach ensures the original mesh hierarchy is maintained during the process.\n\n\n**Verifying Structure Preservation**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into how the transformations of each sub-mesh are handled during cloning. It retains the relative transforms. Also, I see the code is filtering out collision nodes, which is key. I\'m now verifying the structure of `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, trying to understand how `selectedSources` works in this specific case. I\'m also considering the potential impacts of merging geometries, which could flatten the mesh hierarchy, and I\'m looking to see whether that is the case here or not.\n\n\n**Confirming Hierarchical Integrity**\n\nMy focus is to ensure the original sub-mesh structure of the GLB is strictly maintained, as the instructions specify. I\'ve re-affirmed this requirement: no merging, and no generic replacements, and the original hierarchical structure must remain. Now, I\'m examining `buildTeamBaseQuadrant` to verify the placement strategy adheres to this strict structural preservation.\n\n\n**Calculating Fence Perimeter**\n\nI\'m now determining the necessary number of fence segments for the base perimeter and gate, accounting for both blue and red base ID ranges (100-199 and 200-299, respectively). I\'ve calculated the fence needs, ensuring no ID collisions. Also, I\'m working with the exact math for the military base perimeter, using bastion center coordinates and radius, ensuring the fence walls connect between the bastions. Furthermore, I\'ve confirmed fence dimensions in local coordinates with `concrete_fence` placed at `rotY = 0`.\n\n\n**Validating Fence Dimensions**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the fence dimensions at `rotY = 0` and `Math.PI / 2`, matching calculated values. With a scale of 1.0, width is 2.0172 meters. I\'ve verified placement on the north wall, calculating fence counts for a 26-meter span. The first and last fence placements perfectly abut the northwest and northeast bastions at `x = -55.0` and `x = -29.0`. This means that there will be 13 fences.\n\n\n**Verifying Segment Alignment**\n\nI\'ve just pinpointed the center coordinates for each of the 13 fence segments along the north wall. I\'ve re-confirmed, and each segment aligns perfectly edge-to-edge as expected! This precise placement confirms the dimensions and spacing calculations are spot on. I\'m now certain of the arrangement.\n\n\n**Calculating Wall Placements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the West wall, between the northwest and southwest bastions. I\'ve precisely calculated the segment count (15) and center positions for each fence panel, accounting for bastion offsets and ensuring edge-to-edge alignment. I\'ve re-verified these calculations, and I\'m very happy with them!\n\n\n**Confirming East Wall Placement**\n\nI\'m now focused on the east wall of the perimeter. I\'ve re-confirmed the precise segment counts and placements, taking into account the gate position and its associated guard posts. I\'m verifying the 6 segments north and south of the gate, and I\'m very happy with their positions. I\'m taking the segment placements into consideration.\n\n\n**Analyzing Gate Placement**\n\nI\'ve just calculated the east wall segment counts and center positions, precisely aligning with the gate\'s edge. I\'ve pinpointed that the edge of the gate is at `z = 42.0`, which perfectly aligns the fence, and the gate opening is perfectly `z = [36.0 .. 42.0]`. I\'m now verifying the symmetry and mathematical precision, and confirming that the entrance checkpoint barricades should be replaced with `concrete_fence` and form security wing walls.\n\n\n**Planning Gate Barricades**\n\nI\'m now determining optimal concrete fence placement to funnel traffic into the checkpoint gate. I\'ve considered angled wing walls and parallel guide walls, and precisely calculated their positions for `concrete_fence` based on the gate opening dimensions. Also, I\'ve confirmed total fence counts per base, with no ID collisions. Now, I\'m checking the collision handling for `concrete_fence`.\n\n\n"
