  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Prop Properties**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `concrete_fence` prop. Its specifications include being breakable, with a mass of 8.5 and a radius dependent on the scale \'s\'. I\'m focusing on the interaction with units: when a unit walks against it, the solid push impulse prevents passage. I\'m also considering how high-speed tank impacts affect its breakability and how to define that.\n\n\n**Verifying GLB Implementation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the concrete fence shatters realistically with debris above a certain impact threshold! I\'m now examining the GLB asset, `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, to ensure its visual representation is correct upon spawning. Specifically, I\'m checking the `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` function within `src/game/lyoAssetsAndShaders.ts` and focusing on the handling of reference nodes and potential sub-mesh issues. The intention is to guarantee the correct visual representation in the world.\n\n\n**Investigating Reference Nodes**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the nodes within `concrete-fence-1a.glb`, specifically those related to `ConcreteFence_1A.REF`. I\'ve confirmed it houses the `Plane.20009` mesh. I\'m recalling the Blender structure, remembering that `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` serves as the reference point, while other numbered nodes held original placement information from testing. The `refNodes` selection focuses on the cleaner reference model.\n\n\n**Examining Child Meshes**\n\nI\'m checking if `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` in the GLB asset has multiple meshes, and it appears it only has one actual mesh, which is `cuboid_fixed.009`. It is a child of the `REF` node. I am looking at how `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` handles meshes, to make sure this is correctly handled.\n\n\n**Confirming Traversal Behavior**\n\nI\'ve just verified how the `source.traverse` method operates in this context. It confirms that the `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` mesh is visited by the traversal function. The important aspect is that the sub-mesh structure of `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` is preserved, with each mesh retaining its individual geometry and material. This is crucial for the correct visualization.\n\n\n**Clarifying Sub-Mesh Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on preserving the original sub-mesh structure of `concrete-fence-1a.glb`. The prompt\'s emphasis on \"tetap mempertahankan struktur sub-mesh aslinya\" suggests a potential issue with mesh merging or flattening. I\'m considering why the user would specifically request this, suspecting it\'s to avoid the loss of individual fence segment characteristics like materials, UVs, textures, and destructibility, which might occur with geometry merging, or the loss of the submesh hierarchy which is also undesirable.\n\n\n**Confirming Prop Instantiation**\n\nI\'ve checked and confirmed the `concrete_fence` is indeed instantiated through `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb`. Specifically, looking at `FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx`, lines 1320-1340, it appears within the `gltfMap` object, associating the `concrete_fence` kind with the `concreteFenceGltf` asset. This confirms the expected instantiation path.\n\n\n"
