    thinking: "**Inspecting Browser Rendering**\n\nI\'ve got a clean compile, which is great! Now I\'m shifting focus to rendering. I\'m considering using `playwright-cli` or `computer-use` to see the browser output. However, before I do that, I\'m going to take a look at `FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx`, specifically lines 180 to 240, to examine the current state of `buildTeamBaseQuadrant`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Perimeter Logic**\n\nOkay, I\'m examining the perimeter wall construction within `FactoryPlaygroundCanvas.tsx`. Lines 180-240 specifically define the placement logic for the concrete fence pieces. Currently, I\'m reviewing the coordinate assignments and transformations to understand how the rectangular fortified perimeter is generated. It\'s clear how the north, south, and west walls are defined, and I\'m currently parsing the east wall logic.\n\n\n**Examining East Wall Placement**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specific coordinates used to construct the east perimeter wall, and it\'s clear the security gate has an opening at z = 39.0. I\'m focusing on those fence pieces now, and how they relate to the coordinates of the other walls. After I get the perimiter\'s x,y,z, i\'ll transition to the \"Real Extracted Lyo .GLB Props\" which will be integrated in the masterplan buildings.\n\n\n**Locating Prop Placements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific coordinate values within the code, trying to discern how various props are positioned relative to each other within the scene. I\'m noting specific instances of `place()` calls, like for explosive barrels, wheelbarrows, and lampposts, to get a handle on their x, y, z positioning and scaling factors. I\'m keen on understanding the relationships between the locations.\n\n\n**Revising Perimeter Layout**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the perimeter fencing, replacing the old barricades with edge-to-edge concrete fences around both Blue and Red Bases using the `concrete-fence-1a.glb` model. I\'ve recalculated fence counts and placements: the north and south walls now use 13 fences each, arranged with a 2.0m spacing between their edges. The old spacing for the East and West walls has also been updated, with more fences implemented across all walls. I\'m focusing now on adjusting fence positions for the new arrangement.\n\n\n**Verifying Asset Loading**\n\nI\'m now back-checking my assumptions about how `concrete-fence-1a.glb` is handled within the code, and how the sub-mesh structure is preserved. Specifically, I\'m examining `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` in `src/game/lyoAssetsAndShaders.ts` to see how the geometry gets loaded. Lines 2189-2212 are of particular interest. I\'m focusing on confirming if the sub-mesh structure is correctly maintained during the prop spawning process. I\'ll pay attention to the logic to determine how the sub-meshes are handled in terms of parent-child relationships and transformations.\n\n\n**Examining Sub-Mesh Extraction**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on sub-mesh extraction from the GLB asset. I\'m focusing on how geometry and materials are copied and how the parent-child relationships are preserved within the `spawnRealLyoPropFromGlb` function, specifically lines 2189-2212. I\'m now particularly focused on examining the code that handles mesh cloning and transformation, ensuring that properties are correctly applied. Now, the `cube.389` mesh is the current point of interest.\n\n\n**Confirming Fence Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the sub-mesh hierarchy of `concrete-fence-1a.glb`. I\'ve confirmed that each fence instance retains its full `OrientedRefRoot` -> `Mesh` -> `LyoModelStroke` hierarchy with the original materials and textures. Specifically, I\'ve checked the node names and their relationships within the GLB file and can say that `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` IS the `.REF` node. I\'ve also checked whether this node has children or is the mesh; it does have the mesh. The `Plane.20009` mesh on `ConcreteFence_1A.REF` has primitive 0 with material 0 (`gameTexture`).\n\n\n"
